<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enver Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ismail Enver</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enver Pasha was an Ottoman officer and politician, a leader of the Young Turk Revolution of 1908 and of the Committee of Union and Progress. As war minister from 1914 he took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s side and led the disastrous Sarikamish campaign. After the defeat he fled, and in 1921 he joined the Basmachi rebels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, dying in battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enver-pasha.jpg" alt="Enver Pasha" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicola Perscheid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enver_Pasha_1911.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>İsmail Enver was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 23 November 1881. His father Ahmed was either a Gagauz bridge-keeper in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Monastir</a> or an Albanian small-town public prosecutor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and his mother Ayşe Dilara was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a>. He grew up in Monastir (Bitola) from the age of six and in 1902 graduated from the Ottoman Military Academy, becoming a mektebli, an officer with a formal military education. His classmate there was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> (Atatürk), and the two developed a rivalry.</p><p>Through his uncle Halil Kut he became the twelfth member of the Ottoman Freedom Society, which merged with the Committee of Union and Progress (CUP). On his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Monastir</a> in 1906 he formed a CUP cell there and brought Ottoman officers into the organisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In July 1908 Ahmed Niyazi went into the mountains from Resne and called for the restoration of the constitution of 1876, and Enver and others followed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> bands, helping to start the Young Turk Revolution against Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a>. He and Niyazi were hailed as heroes of freedom. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> invaded Tripolitania on 29 September 1911, Enver went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, mobilised about 20,000 troops and took command of the Benghazi zone, though the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> recalled him and Italy took control of Libya. A coup in 1913 brought the CUP to power and Enver became Minister of War, with Talaat in charge of the civil government.</p><p>He was an architect of the Ottoman-German alliance. Without informing the cabinet he allowed the German warships Goeben and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Breslau</a> into the Dardanelles, and on 29 October 1914 Admiral Souchon bombed Russian ports in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared war. At the Battle of Sarikamish in the winter of 1914–1915 he pressed an attack in the mountains despite objections, and a large part of the 90,000-strong army froze to death or was killed. He blamed Armenian soldiers for the defeat and later initiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> and massacres of Western Armenians, culminating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-genocide">Armenian genocide</a>. In 1918 he planned an Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, halted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sardarabad">Battle of Sardarabad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> dismissed Enver as War Minister on 4 October 1918, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-mudros">Armistice of Mudros</a> he escaped by German submarine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a> with seven other CUP leaders. He was tried in absentia in 1919–20 for plunging the country into war without a legitimate reason, for the forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and for leaving without permission, and was condemned to death. In 1921 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> to help suppress the Basmachi Revolt, but he defected and became the rebels' commander, hoping to realise his pan-Turkic aims. He was killed in 1922, and his remains were reburied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> in 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enver Pasha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Officers made the revolution of 1908: Ahmed Niyazi Bey was its initiator and a leader, with <b>Enver Pasha</b> and Eyüp Sabri.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · People</a></li><li><blockquote>It faced the Basmachi resistance, which in 1921 and 1922 was joined by the Ottoman general <b>Enver Pasha</b>, killed in eastern Bukhara in 1922.</blockquote><a href="/state/bukharan-peoples-soviet-republic">Bukharan People's Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in the Bukharan People's Soviet Republic. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1907" aria-label="Istanbul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5258 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Enver Pasha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1907">What was here in 1907? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
